language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ Partition the numbers 1..n into two sets A and B of equal size, equal sums and equal sums of squares.
given n : int(1..100)
find A : matrix indexed by [int(1..n / 2)] of int(1..n)
find B : matrix indexed by [int(1..n / 2)] of int(1..n)

branching on [A, B]

such that
$ every number 1..n is in exactly one of the two sets (all entries of A and B together are distinct)
allDiff(cat(A, B)),
$ the entries of A are listed in increasing order (sets have no order; this removes only permutations within A)
forAll i : int(1..n / 2 - 1) . A[i] < A[i + 1],
$ the entries of B are listed in increasing order (sets have no order; this removes only permutations within B)
forAll i : int(1..n / 2 - 1) . B[i] < B[i + 1],
$ implied: the total of 1..n is n(n+1)/2, so each set sums to half of it
(sum i : int(1..n / 2) . A[i]) = (n * (n + 1) / 4),
$ implied: the sum of squares of 1..n is n(n+1)(2n+1)/6, so each set has half of it
(sum i : int(1..n / 2) . A[i] * A[i]) = (n * (n + 1) * (2 * n + 1) / 12),
$ the sum of the numbers in A equals the sum of the numbers in B
(sum i : int(1..n / 2) . A[i]) = (sum i : int(1..n / 2) . B[i]),
$ the sum of the squares of the numbers in A equals the sum of the squares of the numbers in B
(sum i : int(1..n / 2) . A[i] * A[i]) = (sum i : int(1..n / 2) . B[i] * B[i])
